  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "The following table shows some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[0,8].$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $5$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $4$ | $6$ | $5$ | $9$ |\n\nUse the left Riemann sum, with the four subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area $A$ under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[0,8].$ \n\n$A≈$ \n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA≈f(0)Δ{x}_{1}+f(2)Δ{x}_{2}+f(5)Δ{x}_{3}+f(6)Δ{x}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i+1}-{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $5$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $2$ | $3$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $4$ | $6$ | $5$ | $9$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈3\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 3+6\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6+12+6+10 \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
